{#
Generated by: Claude Code CLI
Date time: 2026/09/10 02:25 SAST
Version: 0.7
Description:
  Email Addresses: the addresses this person signs in with.

  One of the three pages under the Profile pop-out. The owner's rule, 2026/09/10: both addresses are
  login vectors, both are unique platform-wide, and a person cannot change the address they signed in
  with. The primary is therefore shown and never offered as a field - there is no route that edits
  it, and this page says so rather than leaving the reader to discover it by finding no control.

  The secondary is added by verification rather than by typing: a link goes to the address, and the
  address is only added when the link is followed. An address nobody can receive mail at is not a
  login vector, it is a lockout.

  Comments here are plain prose only: no angle brackets, no comment delimiters and no template
  tokens.
Changelog:
  2026/09/10 02:25 SAST
  - Split from the combined profile screen into its own page.
#}
<div class="grid grid-2 section">
	<div class="card">
		<div class="section-head">
			<div>
				<div class="eyebrow">
					Email Addresses
				</div>
				<h2>
					The addresses you sign in with
				</h2>
				<p>
					Both addresses sign you in. Each one belongs to a single account across the whole platform.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div class="list">
			{% for email in emails %}
				<div class="list-item">
					<div>
						<strong>{{ email.email }}</strong>
						<p>
							{{ email.is_primary ? 'Primary address. This is the one you signed in with, and it cannot be changed.' : 'Alternative address. Verified, and it signs you in exactly as the primary does.' }}
						</p>
					</div>
					{% if email.is_primary %}
						<span class="badge permanent">Primary</span>
					{% else %}
						<form action="/account/email/remove" method="post">
							<input type="hidden" name="csrf" value="{{ csrf }}">
							<input type="hidden" name="email_id" value="{{ email.id }}">
							<button class="btn btn-danger btn-sm" type="submit" data-confirm="Remove {{ email.email }}? You will no longer be able to sign in with it.">Remove</button>
						</form>
					{% endif %}
				</div>
			{% endfor %}
		</div>
	</div>
	<div class="card">
		<div class="section-head">
			<div>
				<h2>
					Alternative address
				</h2>
				<p>
					Add a second address you can also sign in with, or replace the one you have.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
		<form action="/account/email/secondary" method="post">
			<input type="hidden" name="csrf" value="{{ csrf }}">
			<div class="field">
				<label for="secondary-email">Alternative email address</label>
				<input type="email" id="secondary-email" name="email" required>
				<div class="help">
					A verification link is sent to the address. It is added to your account only once you follow that link.
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="cl-form-actions">
			<button class="btn btn-primary btn-sm" type="submit">Send verification</button>
			</div>
		</form>
	</div>
</div>
